    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>According to Buddhist tradition, Siddhartha Gautama left a royal Shakya household, practised asceticism, and attained awakening at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, after which he taught for the remaining forty or forty-five years of his life.</li><li>He taught a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a> between sensual indulgence and severe asceticism, and built a monastic order that later took in nuns as well as monks.</li><li>Scholars accept that he lived and taught during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a>, but they disagree about his dates and about how much of the early texts goes back to him.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The oldest surviving written record of the word Buddha is from the middle of the 3rd century BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> pillar inscription names the place as the Buddha's birthplace.</p></aside>
    </section>

