    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">6th or 5th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Siddhartha Gautama is born, according to tradition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, to a Shakya family</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">In early adulthood</span><span class="rf-text">He leaves his home to live as a wandering ascetic, studying under Ārāḍa Kālāma and Udraka Rāmaputra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">After years of striving</span><span class="rf-text">He attains awakening beneath a pipal tree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Soon after the awakening</span><span class="rf-text">He teaches the first sermon to five ascetics in the Deer Park at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, and the first saṅgha forms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">During his teaching years</span><span class="rf-text">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> gives the community a bamboo grove, and Anāthapiṇḍika gives Jeta's grove</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">In later years</span><span class="rf-text">Mahāprajāpatī Gautamī is ordained after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananda">Ānanda</a>'s request, founding the order of nuns</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">At about eighty</span><span class="rf-text">He dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a> after a meal offered by the blacksmith Cunda, and is cremated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s pillar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> records the site as the Buddha's birthplace</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maya-mother-of-the-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maya-mother-of-the-buddha.jpg" alt="Maya (mother of the Buddha)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maya-mother-of-the-buddha">Maya (mother of the Buddha)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maya_Devi-MA_1779-IMG_8431-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rama, CC BY-SA 3.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buddha is known by a clan name, Gautama (Pali: Gotama), a given name, Siddhārtha, and the title Buddha, 'the awakened one'. His dates are uncertain. A Sri Lankan long chronology gives 624–544 BCE, while most scholars who accept the long chronology but date Asoka's coronation around 268 BCE give 566–486 BCE. A short chronology from Indian sources gives 448–368 BCE, and many early 20th-century historians used 563–483 BCE.</p><p>According to Buddhist tradition, he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a>, among the Shakyas, a small community on the edge of the eastern Indian world. Later biographies call his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suddhodana">Śuddhodana</a> a king and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maya-mother-of-the-buddha">Maya</a> a princess from Devdaha. Many scholars think Śuddhodana was an aristocrat, perhaps an elected chief, of a republic and not a hereditary monarch.</p><p>The early texts say little about his youth. In them he recalls a very spoilt life in his parents' home. Several schools and later accounts give him a wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodhara">Yasodhara</a>, and a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rahula">Rāhula</a>. The Shakyas were annexed by the growing Kingdom of Kosala during his lifetime.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;The Buddha&#8221; cites where it covers Birth and early life:</p><ul><li>Rupert, M.L. Gethin, Foundations of Buddhism (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>Geoffrey Samuel, The Origins of Yoga and Tantra. Indic Religions to the Thirteenth Century (Cambridge University Press, 2010)</li><li>Akira Hirakawa, A History of Indian Buddhism: From Śākyamuni to Early Mahāyāna (University of Hawaii Press, 1990)</li><li>Damien Keown, Encyclopedia of Buddhism (Routledge, 2013)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/suddhodana">Suddhodana<span>Father</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/maya-mother-of-the-buddha">Maya (mother of the Buddha)<span>Mother</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/yasodhara">Yaśodharā<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/rahula">Rāhula<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya.jpg" alt="Bodh Gaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahabodhi_temple_at_Bodhgaya_in_Bihar_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amitabha Gupta, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early texts give the reason for his leaving: he thought that life was subject to old age, disease and death and that there might be something better. His parents, they say, wept when he decided to go. The later biographies add the story of the four sights, an old man, a sick man, a corpse and an ascetic, which seems to be adapted from an account of an earlier Buddha, Vipassi.</p><p>As an ascetic he studied under two teachers, Ārāḍa Kālāma, who taught the sphere of nothingness, and Udraka Rāmaputra, who taught the sphere of neither perception nor non-perception. He found neither enough. He then practised severe asceticism, with very little food, until his bones showed through his skin. According to the Mahāsaccaka-sutta and most of its parallels, he decided this had not helped him, and accepted food, a milk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> pudding from a village girl named Sujata in one popular story. His five companions left him.</p><p>In Buddhist tradition he then sat beneath a pipal tree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> and attained awakening. The later biographies add that Mara, the ruler of the desire realm, tried to prevent it by temptation and assault, and that the Buddha called the earth to witness.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths.jpg" alt="Four Noble Truths" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astasahasrika_Prajnaparamita_Dharmacakra_Discourse.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Asia Society created the file. Artwork created by an anonymous ancient source., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buddhist texts say that he hesitated to teach, then agreed after the god Brahmā Sahampati urged him. He went to the Deer Park near Vārānasī and, according to several sources, taught the five ascetics the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a>, a path between sensual indulgence and self-mortification. The ascetic Kaundinya is said to have become the first arhat and the first monk, and the first saṅgha formed. When the community had grown to about sixty, he sent the monks out to teach for the welfare and benefit of the world.</p><p>For the remaining forty or forty-five years he is said to have travelled the Gangetic Plain, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, teaching nobles and servants, householders and ascetics. His order enjoyed the patronage of the kings of Kosala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>. He is reported to have taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a>, dependent origination, and that persons are a causal series of impermanent elements without a permanent self. He also taught lay people, in texts such as the Sigālovāda Sutta, about family, friendship and livelihood. The Pali suttas, as Bhikkhu Bodhi notes, do not teach only a world-transcending goal but also how laypersons may find worldly happiness.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;The Buddha&#8221; cites where it covers Teachings and views, Historicity, Core teachings, Homeless life:</p><ul><li>Rupert, M.L. Gethin, Foundations of Buddhism (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>Richard F. Gombrich, What the Buddha thought (Equinox, 2009)</li><li>Tilmann Vetter, The Ideas and Meditative Practices of Early Buddhism (Brill, 1988)</li><li>Johannes Bronkhorst, The Two Traditions of Meditation In Ancient India (Motilal Banarsidass, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajatashatru.jpg" alt="Ajatashatru" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Museum_Sculpture_-_Ajatasattu_worships_the_Buddha_(9217704485).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buddha lived among many śramaṇa schools, among them the Ājīvikas, Jains and the sceptic Sañjaya Belaṭṭhaputta, and the Pali canon often shows him in debate with their followers. On the way to Vārānasī, an Ājīvika named Upaka was not convinced when the Buddha said he had attained full awakening and took a different path.</p><p>He also criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>' claim of superior birth, held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> had no divine authority, and criticised animal sacrifice. Texts such as the Vasettha sutta say that one is a Brahmin by moral action and not by birth. According to Bronkhorst, however, the Brahmins did not occupy a dominant position in the region where he taught.</p><p>Trouble also came from inside his community. His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devadatta">Devadatta</a> is said to have tried to take over leadership of the order, left with several monks, and formed a rival sect, which the Pali texts say King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> supported. They also say Devadatta plotted to kill the Buddha and failed. The Vinaya texts disagree about what Devadatta disputed.</p><p>Later scholars disagree about how much of the early texts goes back to the Buddha himself. Gombrich, Warder and Wynne think they contain material traceable to him, while Vetter, Schmithausen and Bronkhorst regard parts, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a> as a description of liberating insight, as later formulations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parinirvana.jpg" alt="Parinirvana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana">Parinirvana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paranirvana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mahaparinibbana Sutta (DN 16) and its parallels tell of his last year, a time of war, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> planned to attack the Vajjika League. The Buddha is said to have been ill, to have recovered at first, and to have refused to name a successor, telling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananda">Ānanda</a> to live as an island unto himself, with the Dhamma as his refuge. Sariputta and Moggallana had died shortly before.</p><p>He ate his last meal as an offering from the blacksmith Cunda and fell violently ill. The texts do not blame the meal for his sickness, and they disagree about what it was: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition generally says some kind of pork, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> tradition a truffle or other mushroom, and modern scholars are divided. Mettanando and von Hinüber argue that he died of mesenteric infarction.</p><p>He went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>, where Ānanda prepared a resting place in a grove of sal trees. He ordained one last novice, Subhadda, repeated that the Dhamma and Vinaya should be the community's teacher, and asked if anyone had doubts. His reported last words are: 'All saṅkhāras decay. Strive for the goal with diligence.' Buddhists hold that he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana">parinirvana</a>, final release. The Mallians honoured his body, which was cremated after Mahākassapa arrived, and the relics were divided among several north Indian kingdoms and placed in stupas.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;The Buddha&#8221; cites where it covers Later years, Last days and parinirvana:</p><ul><li>Hans Wolfgang Schumann, The Historical Buddha: The Times, Life, and Teachings of the Founder of Buddhism (Motilal Banarsidass, 2003)</li><li>Alexander Wynne, The Origin of Buddhist Meditation (Routledge, 2007)</li><li>Rupert, M.L. Gethin, Foundations of Buddhism (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhism.jpg" alt="Buddhism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamakura_Budda_Daibutsu_front_1885.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dirk Beyer, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His teachings, passed down orally, were gathered into the Vinaya Piṭaka, the monastic code, and the Sūtra Piṭaka of discourses. Later generations added Abhidharma treatises, biographies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> of his past lives and the Mahāyāna sūtras. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> developed into the Theravāda, Mahāyāna and Vajrayāna traditions and spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It declined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and largely disappeared there after the 8th century CE, while it grew more prominent in Southeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><p>The traditions see him differently. The earliest texts do not describe him as omniscient or as an eternal transcendent being. Mahāyāna texts present Śākyamuni as a cosmic being whose birth and death were skilful teaching devices. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> countries celebrate the day of his birth, awakening and death as Vesak, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and India it is Buddha Purnima.</p><p>Other religions have placed him in their own histories. He was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> as the 9th avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. The Christian saint Josaphat is based on him. Muslim writers called him Budd, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> saw him as a monotheistic prophet. The Baháʼí Faith regards him as one of the Manifestations of God, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> listed him among the prophets before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>.</p><p>His early images were aniconic: an empty throne, footprints, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> wheel or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">Bodhi tree</a>. Images in human form became popular after the first century CE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, taught in the Gangetic Plain in the 6th or 5th century BCE and built a monastic order. Scholars accept that he lived and taught under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a>, but dispute his dates and how much of the early texts reflects his own teaching.</em></p>

